## 5. Ownership/custody: stale and incomplete anchor declaration

Current ownership (`:152922-152938`):

```json
"ownership": {
  "anchors": {
    "dnsValidator": [
      "root-ksk-g1, trusted out-of-band in resolver-public-v1"
    ]
  },
  ...
}
```

This does not match the selected two-client profile:

- `setup.clients.clientB.anchors = ["child-csk-g1"]` and `trustStore = "clientB-private-v1"` (`:152429-152432`).
- `trust-stores-do-not-merge` declares `clientB-private-v1.anchors = ["child-csk-g1"]` (`:153087-153091`).
- The secure clientB outcome depends on that anchor; it is provisioned out-of-band, not via the conditional forward.
- No other field states who provisioned/accepted `child-csk-g1` for clientB. `typedRoots.evidenceRefs` explicitly forbids inferring ownership/authentication from evidence availability.
- Sibling DNS22 private-anchor cases list `child-csk-g1` in `ownership.anchors` (case 0 `:149543-149545`; case 2 `:151559-151561`; case 4 `:154262-154264`). This case is the outlier.
- The receipt erratum `DNS22-CLIENT-CACHE-01` says clientA must use `clientA-public-v1`, not `resolver-public-v1`; the single ownership string therefore does not declare the clientA trust-store scope either.
- RFC 5011 §8.1 distinguishes zone-owner key creation/distribution from resolver-owner acceptance and permits manual/out-of-band updates. `ownership` is the resolver-side acceptance/custody declaration; `signerOnlyPrivateKeys` remains the signer-side custody declaration.

This is a genuine custody/incompleteness contradiction, not a valid alternative topology. An additive input-only erratum is justified to declare the two client-scoped anchors while preserving the existing base anchor entry.

